So after opening help via Enter, then press Tab once: expect focus on #help-close. Then press Tab again: still expect focus on #help-close (meaning focus should stay within the panel, looping maybe?). Actually typical offcanvas: When opened, focus should be trapped inside. First tab goes to first focusable element inside (maybe the close button). Subsequent tabs should cycle within the panel.

Observed failure at step index 3 (the fourth action?) Wait failedSteps array shows index 3: "Tab again without leaving the panel". That corresponds to the fourth action (second Tab). Indeed after first Tab, focus went to #amount (the input outside the panel) incorrectly. So the focus trap is not working: after opening offcanvas, first Tab moved focus to #amount (outside) instead of staying inside.

Thus the issue: The offcanvas focus trap is not activating properly, or the trap element is not set correctly.

Looking at offcanvas.js: In show method, after backdrop show, if !this._config.scroll then this._focustrap.activate(). The default config scroll: false (see Default: { backdrop: true, keyboard: true, scroll: false }). So scroll is false, thus _focustrap.activate() should be called.

But perhaps the trapElement is not set correctly. In _initializeFocusTrap(): returns new FocusTrap({ trapElement: this._element }). This sets trapElement to the offcanvas element itself (the div#help). However, the focusable children inside include #help-text (p) and #help-close (button). The focus trap should trap focus within those.

But why did focus go to #amount? Possibly because the offcanvas element itself is not focusable (tabindex -1 by default due to tabindex="-1" attribute on the offcanvas div). Indeed in HTML: <div class="offcanvas offcanvas-end" data-bs-scroll="true" data-bs-backdrop="true" tabindex="-1" id="help" aria-labelledby="help-title">. So the offcanvas element has tabindex="-1", making it not focusable via tab. The focus trap's activate() method: if autofocus true, it calls this._config.trapElement.focus(). That would focus the offcanvas element (which has tabindex -1, but programmatic focus works). Then it sets up listeners for focusin and keydown.tab.

When user presses Tab, the focusin handler runs: It checks if event.target is document or trapElement or trapElement contains target. If not, it finds focusable children via SelectorEngine.focusableChildren(trapElement). If none, trapElement.focus(). Otherwise, depending on lastTabNavDirection, focuses either first or last.

Now, after activating, the trapElement (offcanvas) is focused (programmatically). Then user presses Tab: shiftKey? No, just Tab forward. So lastTabNavDirection becomes forward. The focusin handler: event.target is the offcanvas element (since it was focused). Since event.target === trapElement, the early return occurs (if event.target === trapElement). So it returns without moving focus. Then the keydown handler sets lastTabNavDirection to forward. But focus hasn't changed. Then after keydown, the browser's default tab behavior will move focus to the next focusable element after the currently focused element in DOM order. Since the offcanvas element is not focusable via tab (tabindex -1), but it currently has focus (programmatically). When user presses Tab, the browser will look for the next focusable element following the offcanvas element in DOM order. The offcanvas element is placed after the button? Let's examine DOM order: In body, we have main container, then button #open-help, then the offcanvas div #help, then form etc. So after the offcanvas div, the next focusable element is the #amount input (since it's a form control). Hence focus moves to #amount, which is observed.

Thus the problem: The focus trap should prevent the browser from moving focus outside when Tab is pressed while focus is on the trap element (which is not naturally tabbable). The trap's focusin handler should intercept when focus moves away from trapElement and redirect back inside. However, the current logic only redirects when focusin event target is not trapElement nor document nor inside trapElement. But when focus moves from trapElement to #amount, the focusin event target is #amount, which is not trapElement nor inside trapElement, so it should go into the else branch and focus the first or last focusable child based on lastTabNavDirection. However, note that the focusin handler is called after focus has changed? Actually focusin fires before focus changes? According to spec, focusin fires before the element receives focus, bubbling. So when Tab is pressed, focus moves from trapElement to #amount. The focusin event on document will have target #amount (the new target). At that moment, trapElement does not contain #amount, and target is not document nor trapElement, so it goes to else branch. It computes elements = SelectorEngine.focusableChildren(trapElement). Those are the focusable children inside offcanvas: likely #help-close (button) and maybe #help-text? p is not focusable unless tabindex. So elements array contains #help-close button. Then if this._lastTabNavDirection === TAB_NAV_BACKWARD, focus last; else focus first. Since we just pressed Tab forward, lastTabNavDirection is forward, so it should focus elements[0] i.e., #help-close. That would bring focus back inside. Why didn't it happen? Possibly because the focusin handler is not being called due to the event being stopped? Or maybe the focus trap is not activated because scroll config is true? Wait default scroll: false, but the offcanvas element in HTML has data-bs-scroll="true". That attribute might be used to configure the Offcanvas instance. In the Data API, they read config from the element. The Offcanvas constructor reads config via _getConfig which merges from element attributes. The Default sets scroll: false, but if element has data-bs-scroll="true", then config.scroll becomes true. Let's verify: In offcanvas.js, they import Config from './util/config'. The BaseComponent extends Config. The _getConfig merges config from element. So if the element has data-bs-scroll="true", then config.scroll will be true, overriding default false. Indeed the HTML has data-bs-scroll="true". Therefore scroll is true, causing the condition `if (!this._config.scroll)` to be false, so `_focustrap.activate()` is NOT called. Hence no focus trap, leading to focus escaping.

